WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.150
The plan follows the workbook's program
model: seven phases and six gates.

2
00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:09.562
It runs from pre-flight, through
foundation, a pilot division,

3
00:00:09.562 --> 00:00:14.238
general rollout and legacy
retirement, to retiring AD DS itself.

4
00:00:14.238 --> 00:00:18.513
Then comes steady state, which has
no gate; it's a standing rhythm.

5
00:00:18.513 --> 00:00:22.290
Each gate has a plain name, like
Pilot Clean or Legacy Quiet,

6
00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.100
and the slide shows them all.

7
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.826
Every phase is bound by
the same constraints:

8
00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:31.172
change starts at the authoritative
source and closes with provenance,

9
00:00:31.172 --> 00:00:34.560
privilege is time-bound,
copies stay non-authoritative,

10
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.875
drift stays visible, and
nothing needs rip-and-replace.

11
00:00:37.875 --> 00:00:41.275
Underneath is one rule:
assess before you act.

12
00:00:41.275 --> 00:00:46.026
The master plan's illustrative fifty-two
weeks stop short of retiring AD DS,

13
00:00:46.026 --> 00:00:49.475
and large federal estates
usually take several years.

14
00:00:49.475 --> 00:00:51.958
Five workstreams run in parallel,

15
00:00:51.958 --> 00:00:56.125
and one dependency never
relaxes: PKI waits for identity.

16
00:00:56.125 --> 00:01:00.340
The gates, not the calendar,
decide when the next phase starts.
